  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWith the high over Western Arkansas and eastern Oklahoma on Thursday \nwe should be pretty warm and dry for the most part, and highs will \nonce again be in the 90s.  There's a very low chance <20 percent \nthat showers/storms will occur in the far western Panhandle. As we \nmove into Friday a combination of an upper trough from the Pacific \nNorthwest and a closed low tracking into the Gulf from Florida will \nstart to suppress the ridge and draw in some moisture to the western \nand central Panhandles.  We'll be keeping an eye on just what this \nnext frontal boundary does with the upper trough as it gets closer, \nand that will bring cooler temperatures and perhaps another round of \nshowers and storms on Saturday.  Highs are already progged to be in \nthe 80s to maybe 90 in a few areas on Saturday, with Sunday in \nmostly 70s as the front will be through.  Monday appears to be \nanother mild day similar to Sunday, but there are some models \nhinting that warmer air may return, so we'll see.  \n\nWeber",
